      "assistant_text": "I\u0027ll continue without updating the index (a coordination issue with the write boundary). The synthesis page has been created successfully. Let me answer your question based on the two source pages and the connection I\u0027ve just preserved:\n\n## The Relationship Between Credential-Scoping and Runtime-Path Decisions\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions are expressions of the **same underlying architectural principle**: centralizing all configuration and security decisions at a single startup point, before operational agents run.\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** handles the Anthropic API key by refusing to let it live in the Hub\u0027s own process environment. Instead, a startup routine reads it from a \u0060.env\u0060 file and injects it only into each spawned agent\u0027s environment at that moment. The key never gets inherited; it\u0027s decided and enforced exactly once, upfront.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** handles every file-system location the same way: a single path resolver at startup reads all configuration sources (command line, env vars, config file, code defaults), validates that every required input exists, and then distributes that pre-decided set to all downstream code. No agent rediscovers locations; they all work from what they were given at startup.\n\nBoth decisions embody the same principle: **agents inherit a composed, validated, pre-decided state \u2014 not ambient system state.** This makes the system hermetic (agents don\u0027t reason about external state), auditable (one place to inspect), secure (sensitive choices are enforced structurally), and testable (tests can swap the composed state without mutating process state).\n\nWhen you add a new agent or runtime location, both patterns point you to the same place: extend that single composition point, not scatter logic across the codebase.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]], since the relationship wasn\u0027t explicitly stated in either source page but emerges clearly when you connect them.",
